# /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/filters.d/dotnet.rules
# Token-saving command rules for the dotnet integration (shipped by ai-tools-integration-dotnet).
# Parsed, never sourced, by /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/filters.lib.sh, which carries the grammar and
# the safety rules.
#
#   match<TAB>action<TAB>blocking<TAB>payload
#
# These live with the integration rather than in core.rules for the same reason its session-env
# fragment does: they are .NET knowledge, and they install and are removed with the package that
# has it.
#
# The SDK's verbosity is the whole savings here, and it has no environment-variable form -- the
# per-project restore chatter and the target summary are MSBuild console-logger settings, so they
# can only be set per invocation. `-v q` keeps errors and warnings, which is everything the agent
# acts on. The banner is left to DOTNET_NOLOGO (set globally by session-env.d/dotnet.env.sh), so no
# rule carries --nologo: it is redundant with that env var, and -- inserted ahead of a positional
# -- it breaks `dotnet run <file>.cs`, where .NET 10 stops resolving the file as a file-based app.

dotnet build	args	-v,--verbosity,-clp,--consoleloggerparameters,-bl,--binarylogger	-v q
dotnet publish	args	-v,--verbosity,-clp,--consoleloggerparameters,-bl,--binarylogger	-v q
dotnet restore	args	-v,--verbosity,-clp,--consoleloggerparameters,-bl,--binarylogger	-v q
dotnet run	args	-v,--verbosity,-clp,--consoleloggerparameters,-bl,--binarylogger	-v q
# `dotnet test` reports failures through its own logger, which -v does not quiet; the payload
# silences the build that precedes the run, where the noise actually is.
dotnet test	args	-v,--verbosity,-clp,--consoleloggerparameters,-bl,--binarylogger	-v q
